WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:04.838
- OK, so I need a definitive
answer on something.

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:07.716
There's no wiggle room.
You gotta say right now:

00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:10.301
Are we ever going to see you

00:00:10.301 --> 00:00:13.013
do the Dunk Contest?

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:21.563
Are you enjoying L.A.?

00:00:21.563 --> 00:00:22.522
- Yeah, it's smooth.

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:23.898
I'm ready to get back home, though.

00:00:23.898 --> 00:00:26.484
- Yeah. It's funny seeing you in L.A.

00:00:26.484 --> 00:00:30.071
because you're not like a big city

00:00:30.071 --> 00:00:33.241
sceney mixy person.
- Nah.

00:00:33.241 --> 00:00:35.744
I like being in the cut at the crib.

00:00:35.744 --> 00:00:38.371
- Yeah.
- In my own bed.

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:39.956
- So then how do you handle, like—here,

00:00:39.956 --> 00:00:43.293
everyone's like, “Where we at tonight?
Where the bottles? What we doin’?”

00:00:43.293 --> 00:00:44.669
Like, how do you kind of handle

00:00:44.669 --> 00:00:47.130
that when you know it's not really
your comfort zone?

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:49.340
- Stay at the crib with my peoples.
- Yeah.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:52.260
- Well, lately we just been
playing Madden against each other.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.680
So that really just kept us
out of the mix—
- Yeah.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.182
- From being outside.

00:00:57.182 --> 00:00:59.059
- When you play, what
team do you play with?

00:01:00.018 --> 00:01:03.813
- The Cardinals.
- Why?
- D-Hop.

00:01:03.813 --> 00:01:05.648
- OK. Are you all friends?

00:01:05.648 --> 00:01:07.942
- Yeah, that's my guy. South Carolina.

00:01:07.942 --> 00:01:10.361
- Of course. OK, are you good at Madden?

00:01:11.279 --> 00:01:14.783
- I'mma let my fam answer that question.

00:01:14.783 --> 00:01:16.868
- I mean, I don't really know
if that means yes or no.

00:01:16.868 --> 00:01:19.412
- They know I'm like 15-1 right now.

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.706
So if anybody wants smoke in
Madden, just hit me up.

00:01:22.832 --> 00:01:24.542
- Do you play anyone in the league?

00:01:24.542 --> 00:01:27.629
- Nah, I just started playing
when we got here.
- Really?

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:31.299
- I kinda, I rock with it
a little bit now.
- Yeah.

00:01:31.466 --> 00:01:33.468
So you're a week
in, and you're 15-1?

00:01:33.468 --> 00:01:35.220
- Yeah. I'm nice like that.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:39.307
- Cuz I don't think you are a gamer
or were a gamer, right?

00:01:39.557 --> 00:01:41.810
- Nah. I mean, I play, but not as much.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:42.811
I play Call of Duty.

00:01:42.811 --> 00:01:45.313
- Yeah.
- But, like, I don't play the game,

00:01:45.313 --> 00:01:49.275
like, every day. Probably
two or three times a week, if that.

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:50.610
- Yeah, and it's just caught on.
- Yeah.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.238
- Yeah, well—
- I gotta run around with baby girl.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.448
- Yeah. So you don't really
have time.

00:01:55.448 --> 00:01:57.158
- Nah.
-I'm sure you miss her being here.

00:01:57.158 --> 00:01:58.618
- Yeah, that's why I'm ready to go.

00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:01.704
- Yeah, but I love—like, you FaceTime
her all the time.

00:02:01.704 --> 00:02:04.499
- Yeah.
- Yeah. I'm sure she—
- Or she'll FaceTime me.

00:02:04.499 --> 00:02:06.626
- I was gonna say, she probably knows
how to work it.
- Yeah.

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:09.212
She knows how to hang up, everything.
- [Laughing]

00:02:09.212 --> 00:02:13.007
- Put me on pause and say
she wanna watch TikTok.

00:02:13.007 --> 00:02:15.343
- Oh, she's a TikToker.
- Yeah.

00:02:15.343 --> 00:02:17.053
- What does she watch?
- She just like to dance.

00:02:17.053 --> 00:02:19.139
So if she hear any music,
she gonna dance.

00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:21.474
- That's really cute.
- Sometimes she'll dance without music.

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:24.144
She just, I guess, plays a song in her head.
- Yeah.

00:02:25.019 --> 00:02:27.063
Well, you dance without music sometimes.
- Yeah.

00:02:28.106 --> 00:02:30.900
- She, she definitely gets that from you,
I would say.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.070
- Yeah. Just be vibing, you know? Good energy.

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:36.656
- Well, so, like, everybody knows
you are my favorite.

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:38.700
You're my favorite
for a multitude of reasons.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.661
One, because I interviewed
you, like, right before you got drafted

00:02:41.953 --> 00:02:44.539
and you still act the exact same.

00:02:44.539 --> 00:02:46.708
- Yeah.
- Two, because I love your dad.

00:02:47.375 --> 00:02:49.752
That's actually my favorite
person on Earth.

00:02:49.752 --> 00:02:52.172
You're, like, No. 2.

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:55.925
But a huge reason why is
you are, like, one of the most

00:02:55.925 --> 00:02:58.261
supportive people, I think,

00:02:58.511 --> 00:03:00.722
not even just in the league,
but just in general.

00:03:00.722 --> 00:03:03.600
I think especially with women,
especially with Black women.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.602
I think when people have
just kind of gone

00:03:05.602 --> 00:03:08.438
through hard times,
you always very publicly support.

00:03:08.438 --> 00:03:11.065
You've done it with me. You've done it
with Maria Taylor. You've done it

00:03:11.065 --> 00:03:14.986
with Sha'Carri. You've done it with Naomi
Osaka, WNBA players.

00:03:14.986 --> 00:03:17.030
Where does that come from for you?

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:18.448
Because I don't think

00:03:18.448 --> 00:03:21.826
a lot of the guys are like that,
or a lot of guys in general.

00:03:21.826 --> 00:03:24.245
- I can say it's just how I was
raised, you know, to

00:03:24.245 --> 00:03:27.999
just treat everybody
how I want to be treated.

00:03:27.999 --> 00:03:29.792
I'm a very

00:03:29.792 --> 00:03:33.630
caring person, you
know, full of love. So

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:35.089
I just, you know,

00:03:35.089 --> 00:03:38.176
spread love, positive energy.

00:03:39.177 --> 00:03:40.762
It's pretty much me.
- Yeah.

00:03:40.762 --> 00:03:41.971
But you do realize, like,

00:03:41.971 --> 00:03:46.809
how much that probably means to
the people that you are doing it for.

00:03:46.809 --> 00:03:50.480
- Yeah, 100 percent.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:52.357
And now I have a,

00:03:52.357 --> 00:03:54.943
I feel like a bigger voice.

00:03:56.236 --> 00:03:59.530
So I feel like, you know, it—
eventually they will see

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:02.408
it and, you know, more people will
start doing the same.

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:06.788
- Mmhmm. What do you think about the way
it feels like everyone was, like,

00:04:06.788 --> 00:04:09.207
so Team Sha'Carri, rooting for
Sha'Carri, and then she did the race

00:04:09.207 --> 00:04:11.793
and it was like everybody
just kinda turned on her?

00:04:11.793 --> 00:04:13.795
I mean, what was that
like for you to see online?

00:04:13.795 --> 00:04:15.546
- I feel like that's life.

00:04:15.546 --> 00:04:16.464
That's how it is.

00:04:16.464 --> 00:04:18.341
Like, when you up,
everybody wanna

00:04:18.341 --> 00:04:20.760
clap, be
your friend, you know?

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.888
But as soon as
some little thing like her

00:04:23.888 --> 00:04:26.140
just losing a race,
they all just flipped on her.

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:27.934
And that's just how it is.

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:29.769
I feel like if she raced again

00:04:29.769 --> 00:04:33.189
the next day and would’ve won,
they would’ve been back on,

00:04:33.189 --> 00:04:35.275
you know, “Aw, she's the greatest.”
- Mmhmm.

00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:36.526
- It's just, it's just life.

00:04:38.027 --> 00:04:40.446
I like how she dealt with it, but

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:44.367
she's gotta come back 
and redeem herself.

00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:47.120
- The thing that was so puzzling to me

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:50.206
about that whole thing is
everyone's like, “She's too cocky.”

00:04:50.665 --> 00:04:52.625
Like, well, no. She just thought
she was gonna win.

00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:54.585
- Yeah, I feel like she's not cocky.

00:04:54.585 --> 00:04:57.297
I just feel like she's just got
a lot of confidence in herself.
- Mmhmm.

00:04:57.297 --> 00:05:00.174
- I feel like it's the same with me.
Like, I'm not cocky at all.

00:05:00.174 --> 00:05:02.302
Very humble, but I'm confident too.
- Yeah.

00:05:02.302 --> 00:05:05.888
- So if I feel like, you know, I'm
 the best, I'mma say that.
- Yeah.

00:05:05.888 --> 00:05:08.683
- So I feel like that's
just what she was doing.

00:05:08.683 --> 00:05:11.769
- One thing I always remember
when Michael Jordan, you know,

00:05:11.769 --> 00:05:14.772
did “The Last Dance”—everybody's
watching it over the pandemic—

00:05:14.772 --> 00:05:16.399
he was talking about a player.

00:05:16.399 --> 00:05:18.526
I can't remember who, but he was like,
“I respect this player

00:05:18.526 --> 00:05:22.155
because he was still talking trash
while he was losing.”

00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:23.865
- Yeah.
- Like, he stayed consistent.

00:05:23.865 --> 00:05:25.491
He stayed the same with it.

00:05:25.491 --> 00:05:29.287
And I actually think it's, like,
a sign of strength and a sign of, like,

00:05:29.287 --> 00:05:31.914
really knowing who you are that
even at the end of the race,

00:05:31.914 --> 00:05:34.083
she was like, “I'm
gonna come back.”
- Yeah.

00:05:34.083 --> 00:05:38.421
- It's like people want to see somebody
be defeated when they are defeated.

00:05:38.421 --> 00:05:40.506
- Yeah.
- And I don't really know what,

00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:43.968
like, people get from that,
especially online.

00:05:43.968 --> 00:05:46.137
- I don't either, honestly.
- Yeah.

00:05:46.137 --> 00:05:47.472
- I'm with that,
you know, staying

00:05:47.472 --> 00:05:49.974
consistent no matter
if you winning or losing.

00:05:51.309 --> 00:05:54.187
So back to saying,
like, I like how she dealt with it.

00:05:54.187 --> 00:05:56.606
Like, right after she was like,
“You know, I'll be back.”

00:05:56.773 --> 00:05:58.983
Like, “Just gotta put in
more work.”
- Yeah.

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:01.194
- Just simple as that.
- Totally.

00:06:01.194 --> 00:06:04.489
Now, you are someone who loves
to engage on Twitter.

00:06:04.906 --> 00:06:07.992
- [Laughing]
- You will talk to the fans.

00:06:07.992 --> 00:06:10.328
I think at one point in
a postgame interview, you were like,

00:06:10.328 --> 00:06:12.705
“That was for you who said 
we weren't gonna win this!”
- Yep.

00:06:12.705 --> 00:06:14.415
- So tell me that story, and

00:06:14.415 --> 00:06:17.043
what happened with that tweet?

00:06:17.043 --> 00:06:18.503
- It's pretty much just, you know, it'd

00:06:18.503 --> 00:06:22.090
be a lot of talking I'll be seeing.

00:06:22.423 --> 00:06:23.674
It really be my mom.

00:06:23.674 --> 00:06:26.552
She'd be trying to
get me off of Twitter all the time.

00:06:26.552 --> 00:06:29.347
Like, don't even, don't
even go on Twitter.

00:06:29.347 --> 00:06:31.849
Don't look at your phone like that.

00:06:31.849 --> 00:06:34.727
No, I was just looking,
and I just, you know, I be seeing people

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:37.480
talking all the time
before games, after games.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:39.607
And I normally don't 
say too much before.

00:06:40.441 --> 00:06:42.360
Sometimes I want to, but

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:44.821
I'll be like,
“Nah, we gotta lock in on the game.”

00:06:44.821 --> 00:06:47.657
Like, you know, just let the win
do the talking or whatever.

00:06:47.657 --> 00:06:50.034
So then after the fact, 
if we win, now

00:06:51.035 --> 00:06:52.412
I can say whatever I want.
- Yeah.

00:06:52.412 --> 00:06:55.331
- So just, like,
they go on there and talk,

00:06:55.331 --> 00:06:56.582
I'mma talk back.

00:06:56.582 --> 00:07:00.837
So if anybody @ me,
better be prepared for a reply.

00:07:00.837 --> 00:07:02.755
- “Better be prepared
for that postgame interview.

00:07:02.755 --> 00:07:04.257
I'm calling out your @ name!”

00:07:04.257 --> 00:07:05.675
- Yup.
- So get ready for the tweets.

00:07:05.675 --> 00:07:06.717
- And all my fans,

00:07:06.717 --> 00:07:10.763
I'mma make sure they get in all 
your mentions, replies, everything.

00:07:10.763 --> 00:07:13.057
DMs, I'mma make sure they all DM you.

00:07:13.057 --> 00:07:14.392
- All of it. All of it.

00:07:14.392 --> 00:07:18.479
I know. OK, so for me, I remember
when I first started in my career,

00:07:18.896 --> 00:07:22.108
to be completely honest, I, like, 
really cared about social media.
- Yeah.

00:07:22.108 --> 00:07:26.195
- Like, I really cared about what someone
was tweeting about me or commenting.

00:07:26.195 --> 00:07:28.448
You reach a point
where, like, you stop caring, right?

00:07:28.448 --> 00:07:31.868
But I think that
when there's so many people

00:07:31.868 --> 00:07:35.997
always saying things about you, it's
hard to completely block it off.
- Yeah.

00:07:35.997 --> 00:07:38.332
- Are there times that it does
somewhat get to you, or

00:07:38.332 --> 00:07:41.252
are you completely
used to it at this point?

00:07:41.252 --> 00:07:45.089
- I feel like sometimes,
you know, like, it gets under my skin.

00:07:45.089 --> 00:07:47.341
So that's when I'll say something.

00:07:48.843 --> 00:07:49.802
- Mmhmm.
- But most of the time

00:07:49.802 --> 00:07:53.014
I'll just be cool. And, like, it's hard
to not see everything.

00:07:53.014 --> 00:07:56.142
Like, even on my Twitter,
if I scroll for a minute,

00:07:56.142 --> 00:07:58.144
it'll have, like,
"Ja Morant" right there,

00:07:58.144 --> 00:08:00.938
and it'll just show all the tweets
that got my name in it.

00:08:00.938 --> 00:08:04.233
So, like—
- OK.
- I don't know how to get away from that.

00:08:04.233 --> 00:08:06.986
So, like, I'mma eventually,
you know, see the tweet or whatever.

00:08:06.986 --> 00:08:09.864
But, like, no, I really don't care.

00:08:09.864 --> 00:08:13.326
I feel like now social media 
is just brainwashing people

00:08:13.326 --> 00:08:15.620
- Mmhmm.
- Like, people living lives, you know,

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:17.663
they're not
living in real life.

00:08:17.663 --> 00:08:19.957
- Yeah.
- They just go on there
just to say something

00:08:19.957 --> 00:08:22.585
negative just to
get attention, so.

00:08:22.585 --> 00:08:25.171
- And like, why not be nice?
- Yeah.

00:08:25.171 --> 00:08:28.007
- I'm like, "Everyone should just be nice
to everyone all the time."

00:08:28.007 --> 00:08:31.802
Like, what do you get out of
being mean to somebody online?

00:08:31.802 --> 00:08:33.262
Like, I just—I don't understand.

00:08:33.262 --> 00:08:34.889
Like, what would be the point
of making a comment

00:08:34.889 --> 00:08:37.058
that is at least neutral, right?
- Yeah.

00:08:37.058 --> 00:08:40.394
- Like, it's OK to say, "OK,
I don't think you had a great game."

00:08:40.394 --> 00:08:43.397
That's fine.
- Yeah.
- But, like, I just think sometimes people

00:08:43.397 --> 00:08:46.859
tend to take it to another level
that it just, like, doesn't

00:08:46.859 --> 00:08:48.110
really have to go.
- Yeah, like,

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:50.446
you see some players get,
you know, death threats

00:08:50.446 --> 00:08:53.032
and all this stuff.
Like, nah, that's too far.

00:08:53.032 --> 00:08:54.075
- Yeah. No, totally.

00:08:54.075 --> 00:08:55.910
Absolutely. I mean, that's
actually life. Like,

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:58.538
there's no place in the world
where, like, things

00:08:58.538 --> 00:09:01.958
don't have real consequences
except for online.
- Yeah.

00:09:01.958 --> 00:09:05.086
- And so I just think at some point
there is gonna be this awakening

00:09:05.086 --> 00:09:07.505
for people that decide that's
what they, what they wanna do online.

00:09:07.505 --> 00:09:08.506
- They behind a phone

00:09:08.506 --> 00:09:10.675
so they feel like
they can say whatever.
- Yeah.

00:09:10.675 --> 00:09:12.927
- I doubt most of ‘em say it in person.

00:09:14.136 --> 00:09:15.763
- Yeah. No, like, 100 percent.

00:09:15.763 --> 00:09:17.848
Nobody would say it in person.
- They won't.

00:09:17.848 --> 00:09:18.599
- Yeah. I mean, so

00:09:18.599 --> 00:09:21.561
speaking of, you know,
just absolutely unacceptable things,

00:09:21.561 --> 00:09:25.106
I know obviously in Utah,
your family was unfortunately subject

00:09:25.106 --> 00:09:28.276
to, like, a terrible,
racist incident there.

00:09:28.276 --> 00:09:30.278
I know that your dad has spoke on it,

00:09:30.278 --> 00:09:33.030
but I just wanna know, for you,
what was that like,

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:36.450
and what was the timeline from
when you, like, realized what happened?

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:38.869
Cuz obviously you were 
playing a game—a great game,

00:09:38.869 --> 00:09:42.623
by the way—that kinda ended up
being marred by this happening.

00:09:42.623 --> 00:09:45.710
- Oh, I feel like, you know, 
it was tougher for me.

00:09:46.669 --> 00:09:48.546
Like, every game,

00:09:48.546 --> 00:09:52.216
I know where my people sittin' at,
no matter if we home or away.

00:09:52.216 --> 00:09:55.761
Like, I ask where they 
sittin' at before the game

00:09:55.761 --> 00:09:56.929
so I can look.

00:09:56.929 --> 00:10:01.392
Sometimes my mom will wave
and just be like, "Let's go."

00:10:01.392 --> 00:10:02.977
My pops is normally courtside.

00:10:02.977 --> 00:10:06.188
But, you know, we was away.
I put 'em all in

00:10:06.188 --> 00:10:08.024
the same section
for that reason,

00:10:08.024 --> 00:10:10.610
you know, hearing about
how Utah fans were.

00:10:11.235 --> 00:10:14.614
And I mean, I was—I think it was
second half. I was

00:10:16.407 --> 00:10:17.867
scoring—like, I think I scored,

00:10:17.867 --> 00:10:20.328
like, back-to-back,
or there was a timeout.

00:10:20.328 --> 00:10:21.912
And, like, I just went to look up,

00:10:21.912 --> 00:10:24.582
and I just see, like,
my whole family standing up.

00:10:24.582 --> 00:10:28.085
And then I knew at that point
it was, you know, something.

00:10:28.085 --> 00:10:31.088
So, like, I'm trying to look back,
but I'm like, "Bruh,

00:10:31.088 --> 00:10:34.634
I gotta lock into the game."
But my loyalty, like—

00:10:35.551 --> 00:10:37.053
I felt like if anything had to happen,

00:10:37.053 --> 00:10:39.889
I would probably end up
in the bleachers, so I was just trying

00:10:39.889 --> 00:10:42.016
to just tell
our security, like,

00:10:42.433 --> 00:10:44.602
"Just tell them to just stop and chill."

00:10:44.602 --> 00:10:46.854
But at that time,
I didn't know what was going on.

00:10:47.396 --> 00:10:50.900
So, I mean, it was—I felt like,
you know, my peoples was like,

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:53.819
"Ja, it's easy for you
to say to calm down,

00:10:53.819 --> 00:10:55.905
but you don't know
what's going on," or whatever.

00:10:55.905 --> 00:10:58.240
And then they finally told me
after, and I was like,

00:10:58.908 --> 00:11:00.701
"Wow, I wish I told y'all
to just, you know,

00:11:00.701 --> 00:11:02.995
go ahead and handle
y'all business, cuz—"
- Yeah.

00:11:04.038 --> 00:11:05.414
It's funny cuz
that's what everyone says.

00:11:05.414 --> 00:11:07.291
They're like, "Listen,
it reaches a point

00:11:07.291 --> 00:11:08.751
where disrespect is disrespect."

00:11:08.751 --> 00:11:09.835
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:11:09.835 --> 00:11:13.047
- And, like, me—like, my whole family,
you know, they'll laugh and joke.

00:11:13.047 --> 00:11:14.632
Like, we all good people.

00:11:14.632 --> 00:11:17.134
Like—
- Y'all are.
- Even if we met you

00:11:17.134 --> 00:11:19.720
for five minutes, like,
we gonna treat you like,

00:11:20.221 --> 00:11:23.391
you know, we knew you for a while,
laughing and joking.

00:11:23.391 --> 00:11:26.352
And if you see any of my family
get to that point,

00:11:26.352 --> 00:11:28.604
obviously they fed up, so.
- Yeah.

00:11:28.604 --> 00:11:29.689
It feels like lucky—

00:11:29.689 --> 00:11:33.109
luckily, there has been, you know, a crackdown

00:11:33.109 --> 00:11:35.986
on what is going to be 
acceptable for NBA fans.

00:11:36.529 --> 00:11:38.864
But why do you think fans

00:11:38.864 --> 00:11:42.785
even feel like they could have acted
that way to begin with?

00:11:43.452 --> 00:11:44.662
- I honestly don't know.

00:11:44.662 --> 00:11:47.081
I feel like my fam

00:11:47.081 --> 00:11:49.542
laughing and joking, obviously, but

00:11:49.542 --> 00:11:53.295
they just as confidence—
confident in me as I am.

00:11:53.295 --> 00:11:55.673
So I'm pretty sure they was like,

00:11:55.673 --> 00:11:57.800
"Oh, here he go." Like, "He waking up,"
and they probably

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:00.261
just started getting mad
cuz we was coming back.

00:12:00.261 --> 00:12:02.179
- Nah, you was big wakin' up.
I think that was

00:12:02.179 --> 00:12:03.764
47 points in that game.

00:12:04.515 --> 00:12:06.559
- So, I mean, honestly, I don't know.

00:12:06.559 --> 00:12:08.769
I just feel like,
you know, they feel like they can

00:12:08.769 --> 00:12:10.980
get away with a lotta stuff.
- Mmhmm.

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:13.774
- And most of the time,
if it's an incident

00:12:13.774 --> 00:12:17.236
where it's a fan and a player,
normally the player,

00:12:17.236 --> 00:12:19.697
you know, the one that get punished,
get all the attention.

00:12:19.697 --> 00:12:22.324
So I feel like they just 
felt like they would have

00:12:22.324 --> 00:12:23.534
got away with it.

00:12:23.534 --> 00:12:25.035
Utah definitely,

00:12:25.035 --> 00:12:28.205
when we came back, you know,
showed us a lotta love.

00:12:28.205 --> 00:12:30.166
So we appreciate that.

00:12:30.166 --> 00:12:33.669
- Yeah. There was a really beautiful
moment between your dad and Donovan.

00:12:33.669 --> 00:12:37.715
- Yeah.
- And I know Donovan very publicly on
Twitter was like: "This is wrong.

00:12:37.715 --> 00:12:38.841
This is unacceptable."

00:12:38.841 --> 00:12:41.719
Like: "We're all humans."
We're just trying to play this game.”

00:12:41.719 --> 00:12:44.555
So it was really nice to see your dad
say: "Listen, you're great.

00:12:44.555 --> 00:12:46.724
I'm happy you did that. I'm
rooting for y'all."
- Yeah.

00:12:46.724 --> 00:12:48.476
- What was kind of
the inception of that moment?

00:12:48.476 --> 00:12:49.852
And did you get to talk to Donovan

00:12:49.852 --> 00:12:51.645
and your family get to talk to him one-on-one?

00:12:51.645 --> 00:12:54.690
- Yeah, I talked to a couple
of players on their team.

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:56.734
Even when they came to us for Game

00:12:56.734 --> 00:12:59.945
3, me, like, on my pregame shooting,

00:12:59.945 --> 00:13:01.363
a lotta players came up to me

00:13:01.363 --> 00:13:03.157
and apologized for what happened.
- That's great, yeah.

00:13:03.157 --> 00:13:05.242
- So I—like I appreciated that. And

00:13:06.577 --> 00:13:09.330
my parents obviously wasn't at the,
you know, pregame stuff.

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:12.124
So that was really like
the first time they

00:13:12.124 --> 00:13:14.376
came in contact with a player.

00:13:14.376 --> 00:13:16.962
- Mmhmm.
- And Donovan, you know,

00:13:16.962 --> 00:13:19.757
sent me a personal message
to me

00:13:19.757 --> 00:13:22.551
and my family and then was 
able to talk to my dad.

00:13:22.551 --> 00:13:24.345
And like I said, like,

00:13:24.345 --> 00:13:27.765
we show everybody love.
We treat everybody the same.

00:13:27.765 --> 00:13:30.434
And that's just
the type of people we are.

00:13:30.434 --> 00:13:32.895
You know, my dad said

00:13:32.895 --> 00:13:36.023
even though, like, we lost,
he hope they go all the way

00:13:36.023 --> 00:13:38.692
and win it just for
what they've been doing, so.

00:13:38.692 --> 00:13:40.736
- Yeah. No, I love that video.

00:13:40.736 --> 00:13:42.196
- Yeah.
- It's so good. It's so good.

00:13:42.196 --> 00:13:46.367
OK, so I need a definitive
answer on something.

00:13:46.992 --> 00:13:47.952
There's no wiggle room.

00:13:47.952 --> 00:13:49.829
You gotta say right now:

00:13:49.829 --> 00:13:54.041
Are we ever going to
see you do the Dunk Contest?

00:13:58.671 --> 00:14:01.966
[Laughing]
- I don't think so.

00:14:01.966 --> 00:14:03.425
- Really?
- I don't think so.

00:14:03.425 --> 00:14:05.928
- Now, OK, so we've had conversations
about the Dunk Contest,

00:14:05.928 --> 00:14:10.474
and I know your biggest thing is
about the judging of the Dunk Contest.

00:14:10.474 --> 00:14:13.394
- [Laughing]
- So what would it take

00:14:13.394 --> 00:14:16.605
for you to be like, "OK,
you know what? I will do this"?

00:14:17.314 --> 00:14:19.149
- A million dollars.

00:14:20.776 --> 00:14:23.320
- Best dunk gets a mil.

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:25.865
- They give me a mil,

00:14:25.865 --> 00:14:28.033
and I'll get in the Dunk Contest.

00:14:28.659 --> 00:14:32.329
So until then, I'll be watching
and just

00:14:32.329 --> 00:14:34.790
sending my tweets out for the
judging and stuff.

00:14:35.207 --> 00:14:37.251
But I feel like now, you know,

00:14:37.251 --> 00:14:39.336
you just see so many,

00:14:39.336 --> 00:14:42.882
like, crazy dunks in-game
that when the Dunk Contest

00:14:42.882 --> 00:14:45.759
come, you just,
you don't appreciate the dunk as much.

00:14:45.759 --> 00:14:48.262
And not too many people can do
the dunks guys

00:14:48.262 --> 00:14:49.972
are doing in that Dunk Contest.
- Mmhmm.

00:14:49.972 --> 00:14:52.516
- So I feel like if you look at it
that way, it'll be a lot

00:14:52.516 --> 00:14:54.977
more, you know, 50s.
-Yeah.

00:14:55.436 --> 00:14:57.271
At what point did you change
your mind, though?

00:14:57.271 --> 00:14:59.565
Because there was a time
you were like: "Dunk Contest is great.

00:14:59.565 --> 00:15:01.025
I'd love to do it one day."

00:15:01.025 --> 00:15:02.443
Then I just think you were
kinda watching.

00:15:02.443 --> 00:15:05.195
You were like, "All right, maybe not." Like,
when did that shift happen?

00:15:05.821 --> 00:15:09.283
- The judging. Like, the past,
 you know, two years,

00:15:09.283 --> 00:15:10.701
I was like: "Nah. Can't do it."

00:15:10.701 --> 00:15:12.912
- How would you judge it? If you got to—

00:15:12.912 --> 00:15:16.415
if you decided how they were judged,
who'd judge it, all that stuff,

00:15:16.415 --> 00:15:18.918
tell me what your format
would be for that.

00:15:18.918 --> 00:15:20.628
- I probably would still do,
like, the

00:15:20.628 --> 00:15:23.297
former players to judge.

00:15:23.297 --> 00:15:27.301
But I just feel like, you know,
you just gotta look at it as,

00:15:28.302 --> 00:15:30.554
how many people can
actually do this dunk?

00:15:30.554 --> 00:15:33.057
Or, like, how difficult was it
to, you know, do this dunk?

00:15:33.682 --> 00:15:37.186
Cuz even though,
like, you have somebody

00:15:37.186 --> 00:15:39.396
made the dunk
look easy, it's not.

00:15:39.396 --> 00:15:42.024
Like, it's times where
like, I'd be in the gym,

00:15:42.024 --> 00:15:45.277
trying some of the dunks
and be like, "Bro, like, it took me

00:15:45.277 --> 00:15:49.114
10 times just to"—like, and they go
make it on their first time.

00:15:49.114 --> 00:15:50.616
So I feel like they look at it

00:15:50.616 --> 00:15:54.411
as, "Nah, that was too easy for him,"
but not looking at the actual dunk.

00:15:54.411 --> 00:15:56.121
So they'll be like,
"All right, this an eight,"

00:15:56.121 --> 00:15:59.541
when it should be a 10, you know?
- Mmhmm.

00:16:00.209 --> 00:16:02.294
Like, they actually
just made it look easy.

00:16:02.294 --> 00:16:04.338
- Yeah.
- And that's why they should get the 10. Yeah.

00:16:04.338 --> 00:16:07.800
- Yeah, that's exactly why,
especially if you doin'

00:16:08.133 --> 00:16:09.885
between-the-legs backwards. Like,

00:16:10.844 --> 00:16:13.013
same foot, same hand, between—like,

00:16:13.597 --> 00:16:16.684
you don't see people
doing that all the time, so.
- Mmhmm.

00:16:16.684 --> 00:16:18.811
If you did the Dunk Contest,
in this fictional world,

00:16:19.728 --> 00:16:21.772
tell me three players
that you think would give you,

00:16:21.772 --> 00:16:23.899
like, the best run for your money.

00:16:24.984 --> 00:16:27.236
Like, the best competition for you
in that Dunk Contest.

00:16:27.236 --> 00:16:30.322
- Zach LaVine. Aaron Gordon.

00:16:30.823 --> 00:16:33.158
And I'm kinda in a tossup between

00:16:34.243 --> 00:16:37.287
Derrick Jones Jr. and Zion.

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:39.957
- OK.
- Yeah.
- That would be a fun one.

00:16:39.957 --> 00:16:42.418
- Yeah.
- Hey, maybe one day. Maybe one day

00:16:42.418 --> 00:16:45.212
they fix the judging
and they get the competition.

00:16:45.212 --> 00:16:47.381
We can make this work,
NBA. We could do it.

00:16:48.132 --> 00:16:51.051
You've obviously
had a lotta great in-game dunks.

00:16:51.051 --> 00:16:54.388
Who do you wanna dunk on
that you have not dunked on yet?

00:16:55.889 --> 00:16:58.809
- LeBron.
- Yeah, for sure.

00:16:58.809 --> 00:17:01.937
- I'd probably get that poster
and, like, just put it

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:05.190
soon as you walk in my house, right?

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:07.860
I'd probably have it all over the wall.

00:17:07.860 --> 00:17:10.696
- You open the door—
- Like, just cuz it's LeBron, like—

00:17:10.696 --> 00:17:15.242
- Yeah.
- Even—I remember
watching when Tatum

00:17:15.242 --> 00:17:16.243
actually dunked on him.

00:17:16.243 --> 00:17:18.954
Like, I don't know what
I would have done in that moment

00:17:18.954 --> 00:17:21.790
cuz, like, I can do a regular dunk
and I'll be screaming

00:17:21.790 --> 00:17:24.501
for about three or four seconds
and be behind the play.

00:17:24.501 --> 00:17:27.713
So, like, if I actually, like,
dunk on LeBron,

00:17:27.713 --> 00:17:30.632
like, I probably would get a tech.

00:17:32.009 --> 00:17:34.094
- You just won't be able to handle it.

00:17:34.094 --> 00:17:37.181
- Yeah. Like, it's LeBron.
Like, not even on, like,

00:17:37.181 --> 00:17:40.392
big fan—like, I'm a fan of his 
game and it's Bron.

00:17:40.392 --> 00:17:42.978
But who wouldn't
wanna dunk on LeBron?

00:17:43.395 --> 00:17:45.522
- Yeah.
- Like, that's saying
like back in the day,

00:17:45.522 --> 00:17:49.068
like, who wouldn't
wanna dunk on Jordan? Like—
- Mmhmm.

00:17:49.651 --> 00:17:50.694
It's gon’ happen one day. - That's something

00:17:50.694 --> 00:17:52.029
you can tell your kid about, be
like,

00:17:52.029 --> 00:17:55.407
“Kaari, you know I dunked on LeBron
or Jordan, right?”

00:17:55.407 --> 00:17:56.241
She probably don't know who

00:17:56.241 --> 00:17:58.494
they is right now, but—
- She gonna pull up the tape.

00:17:58.494 --> 00:18:01.789
- Yeah.
- That’s the photo
that's behind us on the wall.

00:18:02.956 --> 00:18:06.710
Tell me about kind of like
the anatomy of a dunk, right?

00:18:06.710 --> 00:18:11.340
Like, for example, how did you know
you could jump over Kevin Love?

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:14.134
Like, at what point are you like,
“All right, I can make this work”?

00:18:14.134 --> 00:18:17.638
- I think it was like a—
I think if I remember the play right,

00:18:17.638 --> 00:18:20.599
I think Dillon was dribbling
and, like, the ball kicked off his foot.

00:18:21.183 --> 00:18:22.309
So when I picked it up,

00:18:22.309 --> 00:18:25.229
a lot of the Cavs players
were, like, chasing after the ball.

00:18:25.229 --> 00:18:29.233
But I picked it up and then all I seen
was the goal, really, honestly.

00:18:30.025 --> 00:18:31.860
And then I went to,
you know, just like,

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:33.403
all right, well, it's time to dunk.

00:18:33.403 --> 00:18:35.572
And then Kevin Love
was right there. So I'm like,

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:38.534
well, it's too late
to try to do something now.

00:18:38.534 --> 00:18:41.578
Like, I can't just jump
and then come down

00:18:41.578 --> 00:18:43.539
like, nah, never mind,
I ain't trying to dunk.

00:18:43.539 --> 00:18:45.874
So I just, I tried it.
- Yeah.

00:18:45.874 --> 00:18:48.919
- I end up clearing him,
but I tried to dunk the ball too hard,

00:18:48.919 --> 00:18:50.170
hit the back rim.

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:52.840
- But even your missed dunks

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:53.966
are like works of art.

00:18:53.966 --> 00:18:56.176
Like, they still look pretty good.

00:18:56.176 --> 00:18:59.596
- I think I'm more known for missing
dunks than making them right now.

00:19:00.514 --> 00:19:04.101
My comments be like
"Make a dunk," or people would be like

00:19:04.643 --> 00:19:06.436
"Land on two legs."

00:19:06.436 --> 00:19:07.938
Like, bro, do you know how hard it is

00:19:07.938 --> 00:19:09.815
to try to do that every time?

00:19:09.815 --> 00:19:13.068
- Yeah.
- I be wanting to comment
and be like, "You know there's

00:19:13.068 --> 00:19:15.821
other players landing the same way
I'm landing, right?" Like,

00:19:16.738 --> 00:19:20.117
I can try to control it,
but it don't work every time.
- Yeah.

00:19:21.285 --> 00:19:24.663
Why do you think your dunks look
so cool, though?

00:19:24.663 --> 00:19:26.915
Like, I saw those photos
that you retweeted

00:19:27.166 --> 00:19:31.295
where it's two different games,
but it's, like, the exact same.

00:19:31.295 --> 00:19:33.463
I'm like, how does
that happen?

00:19:33.463 --> 00:19:35.716
- Probably a turnover.

00:19:36.133 --> 00:19:38.552
I'll be thirsty on fast breaks. Like,

00:19:39.928 --> 00:19:42.973
I don't know. I turn into
the fastest player ever.

00:19:42.973 --> 00:19:45.434
Just run down the court
looking for the dunk.
- Yeah.

00:19:46.476 --> 00:19:49.188
- Even on that picture,
I think it was versus the Mavs—

00:19:50.522 --> 00:19:51.815
I’d have to pull the video up—

00:19:51.815 --> 00:19:54.401
but when Kyle passed it
and I dunked it,

00:19:54.401 --> 00:19:57.321
he was like, "Dang, 12, you thirsty"

00:19:57.321 --> 00:19:58.238
when we was running back.

00:19:58.238 --> 00:20:01.325
And I was laughing at him,
but, like, I just take off running.

00:20:02.159 --> 00:20:04.536
And I just try to
kinda play, like, cat and mouse.

00:20:04.536 --> 00:20:07.289
Like, if I know I have people
ahead of me,

00:20:07.289 --> 00:20:10.709
I, like, run slow so the defense will
try to, you know, go to them.

00:20:10.709 --> 00:20:13.253
And then I just come shooting
straight down the middle.

00:20:13.253 --> 00:20:16.006
And I think both of those dunks
was, like, the same play.

00:20:16.006 --> 00:20:18.425
- OK.
- But just different games. Yeah.

00:20:18.425 --> 00:20:21.511
- Are you, for the person that's, like,
getting dunked on,

00:20:21.511 --> 00:20:23.680
are you a believer
in, OK, make the business decision

00:20:23.680 --> 00:20:26.308
and get out the way
or, like, try to stop the dunk?

00:20:26.308 --> 00:20:27.851
- Nah, I'mma get out the way.

00:20:29.228 --> 00:20:30.646
- Like, I don't
wanna be in the picture.

00:20:32.272 --> 00:20:35.609
- I'm 6'3", 175 soaking wet.

00:20:35.609 --> 00:20:39.488
Like, what I look like trying to jump
with somebody like that.

00:20:39.488 --> 00:20:42.366
I'mma just swipe and just move out
the way, like, “You got it.”

00:20:42.366 --> 00:20:45.160
Hopefully I just
tip the ball before they go up,

00:20:45.160 --> 00:20:47.829
but I think I've been dunked
on a couple of times before.

00:20:47.829 --> 00:20:50.123
- Yeah.
- That's why I—
- It comes for everybody.

00:20:50.123 --> 00:20:51.375
- I wanna dunk on people that

00:20:51.375 --> 00:20:55.212
I know, like—once I dunk on somebody,
like, that feeling of trying

00:20:55.212 --> 00:20:56.880
to get the ball out fast and, you know,

00:20:56.880 --> 00:20:58.131
I don't wanna be one of them.

00:20:58.131 --> 00:21:00.092
So I'mma just swipe.

00:21:00.092 --> 00:21:03.303
Sometimes I might try you, though. If I,
if I jump before you, I'm—

00:21:04.554 --> 00:21:06.848
if I can jump before
you, I'mma try to block it.
- Yeah.

00:21:06.848 --> 00:21:09.768
- But if you already on your
gather step, you got it.

00:21:09.768 --> 00:21:11.645
- Best of luck. Yeah.
- Yeah, you got it.
- Continue on.

00:21:11.645 --> 00:21:13.563
- I might just push
you up like, “Go ahead.”

00:21:15.983 --> 00:21:19.278
- I remember in the press conference
in the bubble after your rookie

00:21:19.278 --> 00:21:21.154
season, I asked what you will remember

00:21:21.154 --> 00:21:26.201
most about that year, and your answer
was not making the playoffs.

00:21:26.201 --> 00:21:29.371
What will you remember
the most about this past season?

00:21:30.080 --> 00:21:33.542
- Similar answer. You know, 
making the playoffs but

00:21:34.293 --> 00:21:35.877
not getting farther.

00:21:35.877 --> 00:21:39.548
It's just, you know—I feel like
we showed

00:21:39.548 --> 00:21:42.384
we're one of the
top teams in this league.

00:21:42.384 --> 00:21:44.886
We can play with the top teams
in this league. I feel like

00:21:44.886 --> 00:21:48.598
just our experience and
just jelling together

00:21:49.891 --> 00:21:51.226
kinda played a factor in it.

00:21:51.226 --> 00:21:54.146
But obviously we still fought

00:21:54.146 --> 00:21:56.481
throughout the series, but,

00:21:56.481 --> 00:22:00.027
um, I really just love, like,

00:22:00.027 --> 00:22:03.864
both years. Even though, like, we lost,

00:22:03.864 --> 00:22:05.949
we all still had the same mindset of,

00:22:05.949 --> 00:22:08.702
you know, well, we gotta work.

00:22:08.702 --> 00:22:11.204
So after we
didn't make it the first year,

00:22:12.998 --> 00:22:14.249
we was like, we gotta lock

00:22:14.249 --> 00:22:16.960
in this offseason
and make it next year. And we did.

00:22:16.960 --> 00:22:21.089
So now it's the next step, which is
getting farther and

00:22:21.089 --> 00:22:24.176
trying to actually
win a championship.
- Mmhmm.

00:22:24.176 --> 00:22:26.345
- I feel like this year,

00:22:26.345 --> 00:22:29.139
guys have been together
a lot in the gym together,

00:22:29.139 --> 00:22:32.059
which is, I feel like is
good for us with our chemistry.

00:22:32.059 --> 00:22:35.979
And we just gotta continue
to come together as one.

00:22:35.979 --> 00:22:40.692
- Mmhmm. And one thing I think
every NBA fan realized about

00:22:40.692 --> 00:22:43.862
the Grizzlies is y'all
do not give up.
- Yeah.

00:22:43.862 --> 00:22:49.117
- Like, there is so much fight, and y'all
seem like y'all are never dead.
- Yeah.

00:22:49.117 --> 00:22:51.161
- It's like you cannot
even take your foot off

00:22:51.161 --> 00:22:53.955
the gas a little bit
because y'all will come back.
- Yeah.

00:22:53.955 --> 00:22:56.291
- I mean, where does
that come from for you guys?

00:22:56.291 --> 00:22:58.960
- I just feel like
we have the right pieces around us.

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:01.963
I feel like we all kinda
the same with that

00:23:02.964 --> 00:23:05.550
underdog mindset. 
You know, never satisfied.

00:23:05.550 --> 00:23:09.638
Like, it's times like we can win
a game, you know, we'll still be

00:23:09.638 --> 00:23:12.099
frustrated with how we played

00:23:12.099 --> 00:23:15.769
cuz we know, all right, this game wasn't
even supposed to be that close.

00:23:15.769 --> 00:23:17.562
- Yeah.
- We just never satisfied.

00:23:17.562 --> 00:23:19.231
We always wanna get better.

00:23:19.231 --> 00:23:21.441
And that's just our mentality
to go out there and

00:23:21.441 --> 00:23:26.113
just play hard, like, no matter
the outcome. Play as hard as we can

00:23:26.113 --> 00:23:28.031
And just continue to fight,

00:23:28.031 --> 00:23:29.783
you know, till that
last buzzer sounds.

00:23:29.783 --> 00:23:33.120
So, each and every game,
that's just how we attack it.

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:35.956
- Mmhmm. Yeah, cuz I think
during the playoffs,

00:23:35.956 --> 00:23:38.917
there were times people were, like,
counting you guys out.
- Yeah.

00:23:38.917 --> 00:23:40.919
- And you were always, like,
no, we're gonna come back.
- Yeah.

00:23:40.919 --> 00:23:41.711
- We're gonna figure it out.

00:23:41.711 --> 00:23:44.589
We're gonna always put up a fight,
always give you a good performance.

00:23:44.589 --> 00:23:46.550
And you guys definitely did that.

00:23:47.509 --> 00:23:49.052
Speaking of being counted out,
you talked about it

00:23:49.052 --> 00:23:51.930
a bit: the Olympics.

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:54.391
How you feel about
the Olympics right now?

00:23:55.350 --> 00:23:57.436
You know, I'm happy
those guys was able to win.

00:23:57.436 --> 00:23:59.020
That's big time.

00:23:59.020 --> 00:24:00.814
I actually watched that game.

00:24:00.814 --> 00:24:03.483
- Yeah.
- That was really
the only game I watched.

00:24:03.483 --> 00:24:06.236
You know, I sent out tweets,
like, asking what was happening.

00:24:06.236 --> 00:24:07.988
People thought,
you know, I was trying to be funny,

00:24:07.988 --> 00:24:10.782
but I really wasn't watching.
Like, I was trying to figure out—

00:24:10.782 --> 00:24:13.785
I just seen USA this, USA that.

00:24:13.785 --> 00:24:15.745
And I'm like, all right,
so what's going on?

00:24:15.745 --> 00:24:19.791
Like—and it's like, all right,
they lost this game.

00:24:19.791 --> 00:24:20.792
They lost this game.

00:24:20.792 --> 00:24:24.796
And that really was the main topic
when they was losing.

00:24:25.297 --> 00:24:26.381
If it was games they was winning,

00:24:26.381 --> 00:24:28.091
and you'd just be like, all right,
they won this game

00:24:28.091 --> 00:24:31.094
and nobody said nothing else.
- Mmhmm.
- Like,

00:24:31.094 --> 00:24:33.305
feel like that's just how it is now.

00:24:33.305 --> 00:24:35.432
But, you know, a
lotta people was trying to, you know,

00:24:35.432 --> 00:24:37.851
make it out like I was trying to

00:24:37.851 --> 00:24:39.478
dis the USA team and stuff.

00:24:39.478 --> 00:24:41.146
But it wasn't that.
- Good.

00:24:41.146 --> 00:24:42.481
- Like, me, I'mma speak my mind.

00:24:42.481 --> 00:24:45.358
I'mma tell you
exactly how I feel. So, if

00:24:45.358 --> 00:24:48.403
I wanted to say something about 'em,
I woulda put the @ on there.

00:24:48.403 --> 00:24:51.406
- Yeah.
- Like, @USA, you this. Like, I wouldn't—

00:24:51.406 --> 00:24:53.617
- @USA.
- Yeah. Like, I wouldn't—
- Yeah.

00:24:54.326 --> 00:24:57.245
- I wouldn't even sugarcoat
it or try to, you know,

00:24:57.245 --> 00:24:59.289
talk behind their back
or nothing like that.
- Yeah.

00:24:59.289 --> 00:25:00.874
- I was just really trying
to figure out, you know,

00:25:00.874 --> 00:25:02.292
what was going on.
- Yeah, for sure.

00:25:02.292 --> 00:25:04.836
And I know you were, like, really happy
when they won.

00:25:04.836 --> 00:25:06.004
And it was definitely a good game.

00:25:06.004 --> 00:25:08.173
Is playing in the Olympics

00:25:08.173 --> 00:25:09.841
a goal of yours?
- Yeah, for sure.

00:25:09.841 --> 00:25:10.967
- Yeah.
- Definitely is.

00:25:10.967 --> 00:25:14.804
- Were you, like, upset or hurt
that it wasn't this time around?

00:25:15.764 --> 00:25:17.307
- Nah, not really. I wasn't—

00:25:17.307 --> 00:25:19.518
I mean, I'm grateful and thankful

00:25:19.518 --> 00:25:22.020
for everything. I know, like,

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:23.647
it's all God's timing.
- Mmhmm.

00:25:23.647 --> 00:25:27.150
- So, whenever my time comes,
you know, I'll be ready.

00:25:27.609 --> 00:25:29.611
I just took

00:25:29.611 --> 00:25:32.822
this offseason
for a time for me to, you know,

00:25:32.822 --> 00:25:36.910
just continue to
get better in all areas of my game, so—
- Mmhmm.

00:25:36.910 --> 00:25:40.413
- I wasn't, you know,
too much pressed on none of that.
- Yeah.

00:25:40.413 --> 00:25:43.917
So, we're kind of in a moment
in the NBA where it's like

00:25:43.917 --> 00:25:48.463
a transition. The new young guys
are becoming the stars,

00:25:48.463 --> 00:25:51.758
and then pretty soon the older
guys are gonna kinda phase out.

00:25:51.758 --> 00:25:53.385
Is that what you feel right now

00:25:53.385 --> 00:25:55.845
as someone in the league,
that this is kind of like the new age?

00:25:55.845 --> 00:26:00.267
- Yeah. I feel like, you know,
we have a lot of talent.

00:26:00.267 --> 00:26:03.520
Any time you mention,
you know, guys under 25, there's

00:26:03.520 --> 00:26:05.814
a lotta guys
you can name. Exactly why

00:26:05.814 --> 00:26:07.399
I'm not about to name
everybody right now

00:26:07.399 --> 00:26:09.150
cuz then they’ll be like,
"Oh, you forgot this person."

00:26:09.150 --> 00:26:12.279
But—
- Yeah.
- I feel like the league is in very good

00:26:12.279 --> 00:26:15.365
hands with the guys under 25

00:26:15.365 --> 00:26:18.577
who are rising to be
the superstars in this league.

00:26:18.994 --> 00:26:21.413
- What do you think is the difference
between a star and a superstar?

00:26:21.413 --> 00:26:24.165
- I feel like
you have some guys who are

00:26:24.165 --> 00:26:26.251
capable of being

00:26:26.251 --> 00:26:28.461
some of the top players
in this league. It's just

00:26:29.421 --> 00:26:33.883
certain little things,
like, superstars do.
- Mmhmm.

00:26:34.301 --> 00:26:35.969
- Like, if you mention, like,

00:26:35.969 --> 00:26:39.973
Kobe, Bron, like, all those guys
just have a

00:26:40.765 --> 00:26:43.727
a different edge. KD.
- Mmhmm.
- I feel like

00:26:43.727 --> 00:26:46.813
they just do what superstars do,
whether it's

00:26:46.813 --> 00:26:50.442
taking on whatever challenge,
bringing their team back,

00:26:50.442 --> 00:26:53.695
icing the game for their team.
You know, certain stuff like that.

00:26:53.695 --> 00:26:55.530
Not saying it's like

00:26:55.530 --> 00:26:57.824
some stars who can't do it.

00:26:57.824 --> 00:27:00.243
It's just, it's a difference.
- Yeah.
- It's a difference.

00:27:00.243 --> 00:27:02.579
- It's like this thing
you can't really put your finger on,

00:27:02.579 --> 00:27:05.874
but you just know it kinda when you see it.
- Yeah. Yeah. Like, all right, he's a star,

00:27:05.874 --> 00:27:08.335
but this guy right
here is a superstar.

00:27:08.335 --> 00:27:10.754
Like, you just know
the difference.
- Yeah.

00:27:10.754 --> 00:27:12.047
So, when we were talking about

00:27:12.047 --> 00:27:14.924
how you would love to, you
know, one day dunk on LeBron

00:27:14.924 --> 00:27:17.052
or like when people talk about,
you know, dunking on Jordan,

00:27:17.510 --> 00:27:20.680
who do you think of this wave
is gonna be that guy

00:27:20.680 --> 00:27:24.059
where people talk about him as, like,
almost the standard?

00:27:24.059 --> 00:27:27.020
- I'mma say me. I just have
that confidence in myself.

00:27:27.020 --> 00:27:29.314
- I knew you were gonna say you.
I should've said you can't say you.

00:27:31.149 --> 00:27:34.361
I should've said
you're not allowed to do that.

00:27:35.070 --> 00:27:37.530
But I mean, I think that—
that's the thing.

00:27:37.530 --> 00:27:39.074
So, when someone asks someone
a question like that

00:27:39.074 --> 00:27:40.700
and then they say themselves,
everyone's like, "Oh, my God,

00:27:40.700 --> 00:27:42.160
they said themselves.
I can't believe they would do that."

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:43.953
But why would you not say yourself?
- Yeah.

00:27:43.953 --> 00:27:44.996
- Like, that's the point.

00:27:44.996 --> 00:27:45.789
- Me personally,

00:27:45.789 --> 00:27:47.707
I’ll never say nobody

00:27:47.707 --> 00:27:51.419
in this world can beat me
one-on-one, better than me.
- Mmhmm.

00:27:51.419 --> 00:27:54.381
- Like, it's just that confidence
I have in myself.
- Mmhmm.

00:27:54.381 --> 00:27:56.132
- And no matter, you know, who

00:27:56.132 --> 00:27:58.301
I'm going up against,
I'mma always take myself. Like,

00:27:59.636 --> 00:28:00.762
I don't fear nobody.

00:28:00.762 --> 00:28:03.223
So, I mean, everybody, like

00:28:03.223 --> 00:28:05.934
I've been saying, put on their shoes
and stuff the same way I do.

00:28:05.934 --> 00:28:07.602
- Yeah.
- So,

00:28:07.602 --> 00:28:10.522
no point in, you know, being
scared of somebody out there.

00:28:10.522 --> 00:28:16.111
I'm taking me and doubling
down every time.
- Yeah. As you should. As you should.

00:28:16.111 --> 00:28:17.529
One last thing I wanted
to talk to you about,

00:28:17.529 --> 00:28:20.657
because we touched on it
a bit with the article

00:28:20.657 --> 00:28:23.159
that I did with GQ
and I had interviewed you for that.

00:28:23.159 --> 00:28:25.662
- 42.
- Very cute. You had your bottle of 42.

00:28:25.662 --> 00:28:27.414
Everybody, that's Ja's favorite drink.

00:28:27.414 --> 00:28:28.873
You ever wanna get him anything,

00:28:28.873 --> 00:28:30.166
get him a bottle of 1942.

00:28:30.166 --> 00:28:34.963
- 1942.
- Cuz you had your 21st birthday in the bubble.
- In the bubble, yeah.

00:28:34.963 --> 00:28:38.341
- And, like, they threw a little party for
you.
- 42 was on deck.

00:28:38.341 --> 00:28:41.386
- Yeah, 42 was a thing.

00:28:41.386 --> 00:28:44.180
And then you very recently
turned 22.

00:28:44.180 --> 00:28:45.098
Happy late birthday.

00:28:45.098 --> 00:28:48.393
I remember the days.
- Thank you.
- Enjoy being 22.

00:28:49.436 --> 00:28:51.104
But no, in the bubble,

00:28:51.104 --> 00:28:54.232
one thing you were talking about was
how really difficult it was for you

00:28:54.232 --> 00:28:56.609
at times.
- Yeah.
- Like, it was difficult for, really, most people.

00:28:56.609 --> 00:28:58.319
And you said that you went through

00:28:58.319 --> 00:29:02.365
a lot of mental issues there
but nobody really knew.
- Yeah.

00:29:02.365 --> 00:29:04.993
- Can you kind of elaborate on
what it looked like for you mentally?

00:29:05.618 --> 00:29:07.370
- Me, like, I'm a big family person.

00:29:07.370 --> 00:29:08.204
So, you know, being away

00:29:08.204 --> 00:29:10.749
from my family, obviously,
was tough on me.

00:29:10.749 --> 00:29:12.167
Any time everybody

00:29:12.167 --> 00:29:14.627
asks me, like,
"What's your hobbies off the court?"

00:29:14.627 --> 00:29:17.297
Like, that's my hobbies. You know,
spending time with my family.

00:29:17.297 --> 00:29:19.549
We’ll find something
to do. Like, it don't matter what.

00:29:19.549 --> 00:29:22.761
Like here, we all just started
just playing Madden just

00:29:23.428 --> 00:29:24.637
to, you know, just bond.

00:29:24.637 --> 00:29:27.140
Like, we just sit outside,

00:29:27.140 --> 00:29:30.518
play a game, and
we're just very competitive.

00:29:30.518 --> 00:29:33.396
But being in that bubble,
you know, I wasn't able to do that.

00:29:33.855 --> 00:29:36.065
I had to talk to all my family

00:29:36.441 --> 00:29:39.110
through FaceTime,
which was—you know, it's

00:29:39.110 --> 00:29:42.238
not a bad thing, but it was tough
not actually seeing them.

00:29:42.238 --> 00:29:44.949
I had a daughter. Couldn't even see her.

00:29:44.949 --> 00:29:47.368
Trying to talk to her
through FaceTime, and,

00:29:47.702 --> 00:29:50.705
I don't know, I just felt like
the bubble just wasn't me. Like,

00:29:50.705 --> 00:29:54.959
obviously I'm a homebody.
I like being at home, but

00:29:55.585 --> 00:29:56.753
just being stuck in there

00:29:56.753 --> 00:29:59.964
and not being able to do nothing
was kind of tough, like—

00:30:00.715 --> 00:30:04.469
I was just overthinking a lot.

00:30:04.469 --> 00:30:06.805
I was, you know, getting down.

00:30:06.805 --> 00:30:10.934
But I don't tell nobody
none of that stuff.

00:30:10.934 --> 00:30:15.438
Like I just—anytime
somebody asks me, like, "Are you OK?"

00:30:15.438 --> 00:30:18.483
I'm gonna always say, "I'm good,"
even if I'm not. I'm

00:30:18.483 --> 00:30:20.902
just kinda used to
getting through stuff on my own.

00:30:20.902 --> 00:30:23.446
So it was definitely tough for me.

00:30:23.446 --> 00:30:27.867
- Yeah.
- Not saying I was ready to leave the bubble, but I was.
- Yeah.

00:30:27.867 --> 00:30:30.495
I mean, everyone was.
Everyone was ready to go.

00:30:30.495 --> 00:30:32.664
But I mean, is that difficult,
like, to know that

00:30:32.664 --> 00:30:34.499
even if you're not OK,
you're saying you are.

00:30:34.499 --> 00:30:35.875
And then it's like, OK, well,
when you're not OK,

00:30:35.875 --> 00:30:38.503
what do you do? Who do you talk to?
What—it feels like—

00:30:38.503 --> 00:30:41.714
- It's not, it’s not—I feel like the world now,

00:30:41.714 --> 00:30:42.757
people say it's toxic.

00:30:42.757 --> 00:30:46.052
I feel like it is. Like cuz,
it can go either way.

00:30:46.052 --> 00:30:48.721
Like, you know, you
probably can get through it, or you can

00:30:48.721 --> 00:30:50.723
go down even more.

00:30:51.850 --> 00:30:52.725
I always say—like,

00:30:52.725 --> 00:30:56.855
one of my favorite quotes was,
you don't realize you're drowning

00:30:56.855 --> 00:30:58.857
when you're trying to be
everyone else's anchor.

00:30:59.774 --> 00:31:01.818
So, like through the bubble
I was

00:31:01.818 --> 00:31:04.571
just thinking about that quote, like,
you know, sometimes you gotta

00:31:04.571 --> 00:31:07.740
put yourself first. Like
your health. No matter what it is. Like,

00:31:08.783 --> 00:31:10.785
I'm always making
sure everybody else straight,

00:31:10.785 --> 00:31:13.121
but sometimes I gotta
worry about me.

00:31:13.121 --> 00:31:15.290
And normally that's the times
when I

00:31:16.332 --> 00:31:20.086
be in a dark place,
when I'm trying to make sure

00:31:20.086 --> 00:31:21.546
everybody else good, knowing I'm

00:31:21.546 --> 00:31:25.091
not good and it just be even more,
you know, harder, so.

00:31:25.091 --> 00:31:26.509
- Yeah. That's one of the things

00:31:26.509 --> 00:31:30.471
I felt like they tried to do
well in the bubble was,

00:31:30.471 --> 00:31:33.224
you know how when you would wake up,
you would have to do the questionnaire?

00:31:33.224 --> 00:31:36.227
And, like, the very last question,
they say is, like, “Do you wanna speak

00:31:36.227 --> 00:31:38.313
to a mental health professional today?”
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:31:38.771 --> 00:31:41.733
- And I thought that was like
a really nice thing that they did

00:31:41.733 --> 00:31:43.401
because it was hard
for so many people.

00:31:43.401 --> 00:31:45.361
And when I've done interviews,
I talk about it.

00:31:45.361 --> 00:31:47.113
I talked to Jimmy Butler about therapy.

00:31:47.113 --> 00:31:48.156
I just talked to Michael B.

00:31:48.156 --> 00:31:50.074
Jordan about therapy.
And they've all said, like,

00:31:50.074 --> 00:31:52.201
"I have tried this,
and it has helped me."

00:31:52.201 --> 00:31:54.120
Michael B. said that he still does it.
- Yeah.

00:31:54.120 --> 00:31:56.497
- Is that something that you would try?

00:31:56.497 --> 00:31:58.708
I think, you know, eventually I would.

00:31:58.708 --> 00:32:02.086
Normally, like I'm just so used to
either getting through it myself

00:32:02.086 --> 00:32:05.965
or just talking to my fam. Or, like,
my close people.

00:32:05.965 --> 00:32:07.258
Like, I don't trust a lotta people, you know,

00:32:07.258 --> 00:32:08.843
with certain stuff.

00:32:08.843 --> 00:32:10.637
So I feel like that's
why I haven't yet.
- Mmhmm.

00:32:10.637 --> 00:32:13.389
- But I mean, right now
I feel like

00:32:13.389 --> 00:32:16.517
my life is good.
- Totally.
- I'm happy.

00:32:16.517 --> 00:32:17.560
I'm enjoying it.

00:32:17.560 --> 00:32:20.396
Just gotta stay right there, you know?
- Yes.

00:32:20.396 --> 00:32:22.440
That's what I was gonna say.
You always have a smile on your face.

00:32:22.440 --> 00:32:23.775
- Yeah, I'm always smiling.

00:32:23.775 --> 00:32:25.193
It's a lot behind a smile,

00:32:25.193 --> 00:32:27.695
but right now it's all smiles.
- Good.

00:32:27.695 --> 00:32:31.616
No, I'm glad. Cuz like, I guess—
you know, everybody always be like,

00:32:31.616 --> 00:32:33.201
"She only wanna talk about when
Ja's doing well."

00:32:33.201 --> 00:32:35.119
I'm like, "Yeah, I do.

00:32:35.119 --> 00:32:36.371
He's my favorite."

00:32:36.371 --> 00:32:39.290
But no. So I'm so happy
you were able to come on and do this.

00:32:39.290 --> 00:32:41.542
But tell me, like,
what's the biggest jump

00:32:41.542 --> 00:32:43.002
you're gonna make next season?

00:32:43.002 --> 00:32:44.212
What are we gonna see from Ja?

00:32:44.212 --> 00:32:47.799
- I'm trying to be an All-
Star, All-NBA team,

00:32:47.799 --> 00:32:50.093
and I'm trying to win a championship.
- Mmhmm.

00:32:50.093 --> 00:32:51.844
- That's my three goals right there.
- Speak it. 

00:32:51.844 --> 00:32:55.264
Speak it.
- Yeah.
- And then maybe with some
tweaks...win the Dunk Contest?

00:32:59.185 --> 00:33:01.229
We'll see.
- We'll see. Stay tuned.

00:33:01.229 --> 00:33:03.231
I'mma keep trying.
